WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
Ship 42 stays healthy and orbit after booster 22 aborted its tower catch over a guidance issue. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 162. Super heavy's diversion, the version 3 capacity jump, and board comments at OpenAI come next. Then the counterpoint and an engineering deep dive. Starbase, SpaceX confirmed, sent starship aloft carrying operational starlink version 3 satellites for the first time. The 124 meter rocket left the pad at 447 pm local time on

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:31.260
on November 13th.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:36.180
Ship 42 placed all 26 satellites at about 275 kilometers.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.540
After insertion, the upper stage flew along coast.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.560
Heat shield behaving as expected

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:43.080
in that first orbital stretch

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.160
and teams will monitor the vehicle

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:47.060
over the next roughly 21 hours

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.100
with early deorbit options still available if needed.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:52.960
Dan Huat said everything continued to look good

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:54.960
with the vehicle and the range for return.

00:00:55.520 --> 00:00:58.000
The first attempt to catch a spacecraft coming back

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:00.220
from orbit is scheduled for November 14.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.080
With the upper stage set to re-enter over the Pacific, then steer on its flaps across

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.060
northern Mexico and southern Texas.

00:01:08.152 --> 00:01:13.312
Super heavy booster 22 turned away toward a designated offshore zone in the Gulf of Mexico,

00:01:13.952 --> 00:01:16.572
and went through a control termination before it hit the water.

00:01:17.172 --> 00:01:21.812
The call originated on the booster rather than from the tower, from its guidance, navigation

00:01:21.812 --> 00:01:27.532
and control system, or GNC, and the vehicle had already begun its landing burn before

00:01:27.532 --> 00:01:32.732
it automatically called off the return to pad 2. It reached the water about six and a half minutes

00:01:32.732 --> 00:01:38.712
after liftoff, a large fireball visible on the way down. Launch commentator Kate Tyson said during

00:01:38.712 --> 00:01:43.352
the broadcast that the news was not with the team it hoped for, and the company stressed that

00:01:43.352 --> 00:01:48.152
launch infrastructure in nearby areas come ahead of any recovery try while these vehicles test

00:01:48.152 --> 00:01:53.372
maneuvers they have never flown before. The booster was the fourth block three version to fly and

00:01:53.372 --> 00:01:57.092
would have been the first of that version caught by the tower if the attempt had succeeded.

00:01:58.090 --> 00:02:02.970
A person filming from the Mexico side of Starbase posted iPhone video of Super Heavy's full

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:06.690
descent sequence, a community capture rather than an official release.

00:02:07.430 --> 00:02:11.730
Orbital imagery from the new satellites went out with the webcasts follow-up, and people

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:15.550
looking at those frames noted the clarity coming down from the deployed star links.

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:21.850
Starlink version 3 spacecraft now require Starship because they exceed Falcon 9 fairing limits.

00:02:21.850 --> 00:02:27.850
Each satellite mass is about 2,000 kilograms, roughly twice the earlier generation, and needs

00:02:27.850 --> 00:02:31.570
the 9 meter diameter and the high payload capacity of the bigger vehicle.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:37.370
Each is designed for one terabyte per second of downlink and 160 gigabits per second of

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:37.710
uplink.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:42.990
So the 26 satellites deployed on this flight represent roughly 26 terabytes per second

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:45.770
of aggregate design downlink once they are operational.

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:50.610
Future Starlink missions are projected to deliver more than 50 terabytes per second

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:55.610
of Starlink bandwidth per flight, multiplying the addition compared with prior launch vehicles.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:03:01.150
The larger bus carries more solar arrays, more phased array beams, and more intersatellite

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:05.970
laser terminals than earlier versions, and that single launch increment supplies an order

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:09.910
of magnitude jump and capacity over prior Falcon 9 Starlink missions.

00:03:11.007 --> 00:03:15.407
Elon Musk took aim at recent OpenAI board decisions in a social media post.

00:03:15.967 --> 00:03:20.307
The post highlighted the board's role in directing what had been one of the world's highest valued

00:03:20.307 --> 00:03:25.767
companies, and it landed amid ongoing public discussion of open AI's valuation and governance

00:03:25.767 --> 00:03:30.627
structure after he has previously criticized the shift away from its original non-profit mission.

00:03:31.612 --> 00:03:36.992
On the policy side, Democratic Senators Jean Shaheen and Chris Coons in a letter reported

00:03:36.992 --> 00:03:43.012
by N.V. demanded SpaceX internal communications and technical records on Starlink and Starshealed

00:03:43.012 --> 00:03:48.932
restrictions for Ukraine dating from September 1st, covering service disruptions on October 13th

00:03:48.932 --> 00:03:55.112
24th and November 12th that could have affected Ukrainian military operations, asking who made

00:03:55.112 --> 00:03:59.872
restriction decisions and whether outside parties were involved, and seeking confirmation that

00:03:59.872 --> 00:04:05.872
materials are being preserved, with a November 30th response deadline. Worth keeping an eye on is

00:04:05.872 --> 00:04:10.932
that SpaceX has previously completed three booster catches, yet the added complexity of a

00:04:10.932 --> 00:04:16.592
same mission ship catch from orbit introduced new failure modes. Resolving the guidance issue on

00:04:16.592 --> 00:04:20.632
future block three boosters will determine whether the double recovery architecture can

00:04:20.632 --> 00:04:25.472
be demonstrated before the next flight window, and the booster recovery milestone now slips

00:04:25.472 --> 00:04:29.232
even as worked toward operational version three capacity continues.

00:04:29.752 --> 00:04:34.012
Clayton Swope of the Center for Strategic and International Studies described the outcome

00:04:34.012 --> 00:04:37.032
as a mixed bag after the failure to catch super heavy.

00:04:37.712 --> 00:04:42.572
Jared Isaacman, administrator of the National Aeronautics and Space Administration, or NASA,

00:04:42.572 --> 00:04:46.892
a, congratulated SpaceX on the launch without referencing the booster result.

00:04:47.926 --> 00:04:52.546
On the AI front, cursor traced a recent security incident to an access key that had been

00:04:52.546 --> 00:04:58.546
exposed by Fireworks AI, Unite AI reported, and then published a detailed remediation

00:04:58.546 --> 00:05:02.246
update outlining the timeline and the steps taken to address the breach.

00:05:02.786 --> 00:05:07.586
The incident affected access controls, but did not involve direct compromise of user data

00:05:07.586 --> 00:05:08.566
or model weights.

00:05:09.186 --> 00:05:14.646
Cursor emphasized that the root cause originated outside its own systems, and the update included

00:05:14.646 --> 00:05:18.186
specific remediation actions implemented to prevent recurrence.

00:05:19.217 --> 00:05:23.937
Putting this payload on ship 42 rather than a Falcon 9 is the engineering reality of

00:05:23.937 --> 00:05:25.197
a volume and mass wall.

00:05:25.877 --> 00:05:31.237
Payload volume scales with the square of faring diameter, so a 5 meter-class shroud cannot

00:05:31.237 --> 00:05:36.277
take more than a handful of fat buses without hitting structural or volume limits, while

00:05:36.277 --> 00:05:41.477
While Starship's 100 plus ton capacity to low Earth orbit is what lets one stack lift

00:05:41.477 --> 00:05:43.057
a full set of the new spacecraft.

00:05:43.717 --> 00:05:48.877
After hot staging, Super Heavy has to flip, then stay lined up with the tower arms inside

00:05:48.877 --> 00:05:52.817
a narrow tolerance while it is still a high speed, high propellant object.

00:05:53.377 --> 00:05:57.697
The loop has to close at higher velocities and heavier propellant loads than this block

00:05:57.697 --> 00:06:02.397
3 version has yet shown at the arms, and the tolerance is unforgiving because those

00:06:02.397 --> 00:06:04.197
arms do not move to chase a miss.

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:09.097
On the upper stage, the heat shield has to take peak heating that scales roughly with

00:06:09.097 --> 00:06:13.377
the cube of velocity while orbital speed is on the order of 8 kilometers per second.

00:06:14.037 --> 00:06:19.817
Then keep the stainless steel skin, melting point around 1,450 degrees Celsius, and the

00:06:19.817 --> 00:06:21.177
tanks underneath it intact.

00:06:21.777 --> 00:06:25.157
Translate that speed into energy, and the load gets less abstract.

00:06:25.697 --> 00:06:30.577
A back of envelope, 1 half V squared, is on the order of 32 megajoules per kilogram

00:06:30.577 --> 00:06:32.617
that has to go somewhere besides the tanks.

00:06:32.617 --> 00:06:37.997
Most of it dumps into a thin shock layer of plasma, which is why a missing tile or

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:39.797
a gap is not a cosmetic defect.

00:06:40.277 --> 00:06:41.877
It is a hole in the energy budget.

00:06:42.457 --> 00:06:46.877
Stainless steel can soak and spread heat after the pulse, which is why the vehicle can

00:06:46.877 --> 00:06:51.717
be a steel tank instead of a composite airframe, but it cannot take the peak pulse naked.

00:06:52.317 --> 00:06:56.697
The tiles take that first hit, the steel is the heat sink that makes reuse a metallurgy

00:06:56.697 --> 00:06:58.417
problem instead of a throwaway one.

00:06:58.417 --> 00:07:03.517
The heating pulse lasts on the order of a few minutes, not the hours of the coast, so

00:07:03.517 --> 00:07:06.957
the design is a sprint for the tiles and a marathon for tank temperatures.

00:07:07.537 --> 00:07:09.757
Get the attachment wrong and you fail the sprint.

00:07:10.237 --> 00:07:13.477
Get the soak wrong and you boil propellant on the far side of the steel.

00:07:14.197 --> 00:07:17.077
Atmospheric interface sits near 120 kilometers.

00:07:17.777 --> 00:07:22.537
From there the shield has to manage plasma, keep tiles attached, and limit a blation across

00:07:22.537 --> 00:07:27.117
a 14 orbit profile before anyone certifies the layout for faster crew entries.

00:07:27.117 --> 00:07:32.877
If you priced only Steel and Silica, the Magic Wand number, a thermal protection system

00:07:32.877 --> 00:07:33.537
would look cheap.

00:07:34.157 --> 00:07:38.517
A back-of-envelope idiot index, how many times the finished shield costs over those raw

00:07:38.517 --> 00:07:43.097
inputs, sits in the tens to hundreds once you count attachment engineering and the flights

00:07:43.097 --> 00:07:44.257
it takes to trust it.

00:07:44.817 --> 00:07:49.757
Version 3 rollout stays gated by those same first-stage guidance accuracy and heat shield

00:07:49.757 --> 00:07:53.017
reliability milestones until the flight rate is routine.

00:07:53.017 --> 00:07:58.237
On the tape, SPCX sat slightly lower off a fraction of a percent.

00:07:59.204 --> 00:08:03.644
Before we go, the first attempt to catch a spacecraft back from orbit is on the calendar

00:08:03.644 --> 00:08:05.464
for November 14th.

00:08:05.464 --> 00:08:08.124
That covers everything worth knowing about SpaceX today.

